L000958	\title{Search for associated production of a Higgs boson and two vector bosons via vector boson scattering at \texorpdfstring{$\sqrt{s} = 13\TeV$}{sqrt(s) = 13 TeV}}
L000959	
L000960	\date{\today}
L000961	
L000962	\abstract{
L000963	A search for Higgs boson (\PH) production in association with two vector bosons ($\PV = \PW$, $\PZ$) via vector boson scattering (VBS) is presented using proton-proton collision data collected at $\sqrt{s}=13\TeV$ by the CMS experiment, corresponding to an integrated luminosity of 138\fbinv. Events containing two forward jets consistent with VBS, a large-radius jet from the decay of a boosted \PH to a pair of \PQb quarks, and 0, 1, or 2 charged leptons coming from \PV decays are selected. The process is excluded at 95\%\CL for observed (expected) values of the \VVHH coupling modifier \kVV outside the interval $0.40 < \kVV < 1.60$ ($0.34 < \kVV < 1.66$), assuming standard model values for all other couplings, thus establishing a novel probe of the \VVHH interaction. Constraints are also set on the individual \kWW and \kZZ coupling modifiers, and on the allowed region in the \kWW-\kZZ plane.
L000964	}
L000965	
L000966	\hypersetup{
L000967	pdfauthor={CMS Collaboration},
L000968	pdftitle={Search for associated production of a Higgs boson and two vector bosons via vector boson scattering at sqrt(s) = 13 TeV},
L000969	pdfsubject={CMS},
L000970	pdfkeywords={CMS, Higgs, vector boson scattering}}
L000971	
L000972	\maketitle
L000973	
L000974	The discovery of the Higgs boson (\PH) by the ATLAS and CMS Collaborations at the CERN LHC in 2012~\cite{Aad:2012tfa,Chatrchyan:2012ufa,CMS:2013btf} marked a milestone in our understanding of fundamental interactions.
L000975	Since then, extensive efforts have focused on measuring its properties and couplings. The \PH self-coupling and the \VVHH ($\PV = \PW$, $\PZ$) quartic coupling, the modifiers of which with respect to the standard model (SM) are parametrized as \klambda and \kVV~\cite{Heinemeyer:2013tqa,deFlorian:2016spz}, respectively, have so far been probed only in searches for \PH pair production~\cite{CMS:comb25,ATLAS:2024ish}.
L000976	An alternative path to access both these couplings is offered by \PH production in association with a pair of vector bosons (\VVH) via vector boson scattering (VBS).
L000977	This is illustrated in Fig.~\ref{fig:feynman}, where two of the leading order (LO) Feynman diagrams are shown.
L000978	
L000979	\begin{figure}[htbp]
L000980	\centering
L000981	\includegraphics[width=0.49\columnwidth]{Figure_001-a.pdf}
L000982	\includegraphics[width=0.49\columnwidth]{Figure_001-b.pdf}
L000983	\caption{
L000984	Examples of tree-level Feynman diagrams for the production of \VVH via VBS, with dependencies on the \PH self-coupling (left) and the \VVHH quartic coupling (right). The corresponding vertices are denoted by the \klambda and \kVV modifiers, respectively.
L000985	}\label{fig:feynman}
L000986	\end{figure}
L000987	
L000988	The VBS \VVH production cross section at $\sqrt{s}$ = 13\TeV is predicted to be $1.99\unit{fb}$ at LO~\cite{Alwall:2014hca}. However, deviations in \kVV from the SM value of 1 can significantly enhance the cross section and boost the final-state objects, thereby increasing the experimental sensitivity to this process. This makes the search for VBS \VVH production complementary to \PH pair production searches, which constrain \kVV within the range of $\left[0.64, 1.40\right]$ using CMS data collected at $\sqrt{s}=13\TeV$~\cite{CMS:comb25}. ATLAS data constrain the same parameter in the range $\left[0.6, 1.5\right]$~\cite{ATLAS:2024ish}.
L000989	The VBS \VVH production is also sensitive to the individual \WWHH and \ZZHH couplings, with modifiers \kWW and \kZZ are only loosely constrained by the CMS search for $\PV\PH\PH$ production~\cite{CMS:2024fkb}.
L000990	Deviations in \klambda determine less pronounced changes in cross section and kinematics. Consequently, the exploration of the \PH self-coupling is left to future work, and $\klambda=1$ is assumed in this search.
L000991	
L000992	This Letter reports on a search for \VVH production via VBS in proton-proton collision data at $\sqrt{s}=13\TeV$ collected by the CMS experiment at the LHC in 2016--2018, corresponding to an integrated luminosity of $138\fbinv$. The search is performed in several channels: all-hadronic, single-lepton, and dilepton, depending on the number of reconstructed leptons ($\Pell$) from the \PW or the \PZ boson decays.
L000993	Dilepton channels target final states containing electrons and muons with charges of the same or opposite sign (SS or OS). Events with one hadronically decaying $\PGt$ lepton are also accepted in the SS channel.
L000994	Final states where the \PH decays into a pair of \PQb quarks are studied.
L000995	A large Lorentz boost is required for the decay products of the \PH and the vector bosons.
L000996	Therefore, the \PH and at least one vector boson decaying hadronically, if present, are reconstructed as single large-radius jets.
L000997	All channels utilize the characteristic VBS signature of two additional jets with a large pseudorapidity ($\eta$) separation.
L000998	
L000999	The CMS apparatus~\cite{CMS:2008xjf,CMS:2023gfb} is a multipurpose, nearly hermetic detector, designed to trigger on~\cite{CMS:2020cmk,CMS:2016ngn,CMS:2024aqx} and identify electrons, muons, photons, and hadrons~\cite{CMS:2020uim,CMS:2018rym,CMS:2014pgm}.
L001000	The CMS ``particle-flow'' (PF) algorithm~\cite{CMS:2017yfk} combines the information provided by the all-silicon tracker and by the crystal electromagnetic (ECAL) and brass-scintillator hadron calorimeters (HCAL), operating inside a 3.8\unit{T} superconducting solenoid, with data from the gas-ionization muon detectors embedded in the flux-return yoke outside the solenoid, to reconstruct individual particles, which are further used to build \PGt leptons, jets, and missing transverse momentum~\cite{CMS:2018jrd,CMS:2016lmd,CMS:2019ctu}.
L001001	The primary vertex (PV) is chosen as the vertex corresponding to the hardest scattering in the event, evaluated using tracking information alone~\cite{CMS-TDR-15-02}.
L001002	
L001003	Muon candidates are reconstructed within $\abs{\eta} < 2.4$ by combining silicon tracker and muon chamber data~\cite{CMS:2018rym}.
L001004	Muons must meet quality criteria based on tracker and muon detector hits, track properties, and impact parameters relative to the PV.
L001005	Electron candidates within $\abs{\eta} < 2.5$ are reconstructed by matching silicon tracker tracks to ECAL energy clusters~\cite{CMS:2020uim}.
L001006	They must meet identification criteria based on shower cluster shape, track-cluster matching, HCAL energy fraction, and track compatibility with the PV.
L001007	Electrons in the ECAL barrel-endcap transition region ($1.44 < \abs{\eta} < 1.57$) or originating from photon conversions are discarded.
L001008	The lepton isolation is computed by summing the transverse momenta (\pt) of PF candidates originating from the PV within a cone of $\Delta R = \sqrt{\smash[b]{(\Delta\eta)^2+(\Delta\phi)^2}} = 0.4\,(0.3)$ around the muon (electron), where $\phi$ is the azimuthal angle, and is corrected for the contribution of neutral particles
L001009	from additional interactions in the same or nearby bunch crossings (pileup)~\cite{CMS:2018rym,CMS:2020uim,CMS-DP-2020-021}.
L001010	Selections are based on the isolation sum normalized to the \pt of the lepton.
L001011	Leptons are classified as ``tight'' or ``loose'' according to the criteria defined in Ref.~\cite{CMS:2020mpn} in all channels, except for the SS dilepton channel, where criteria similar to those defined in Refs.~\cite{CMS:2018rym,CMS:2020uim,CMS-DP-2020-021} are used.
L001012	Hadronically decaying $\PGt$ leptons are identified by the \textsc{DeepTau} algorithm~\cite{CMS:deeptau2022}.
L001013	
L001014	Jets are clustered from PF candidates using the anti-\kt algorithm~\cite{Cacciari:2008gp,Cacciari:2011ma}.
L001015	Distance parameters of 0.4 and 0.8 are used to reconstruct so-called ``AK4'' and ``AK8'' (or large-radius) jets, respectively.
L001016	Jet momenta are corrected to reduce pileup contributions: the charged hadron subtraction~\cite{CMS:2019kuk} and the pileup-per-particle identification~\cite{CMS:2019kuk,Bertolini:2014bba} algorithms
L001017	are used for AK4 and AK8 jets, respectively.
L001018	Further corrections are derived from simulation and in-situ measurements in data~\cite{CMS:2016lmd}.
L001019	Jets affected by detector noise or reconstruction failures are discarded, and pileup jets are suppressed using a multivariate discriminator~\cite{CMS:2019kuk}.
L001020	The missing transverse momentum vector, \ptvecmiss, with magnitude \ptmiss, is defined as the negative \ptvec sum of all PF candidates and corrected for AK4 jet energy scale calibrations~\cite{CMS:2019ctu}.
L001021	
L001022	The mass-decorrelated version of the \Xtobb \ParticleNet tagger~\cite{Qu:2019gqs} is used to identify \Htobb candidates with a mass-independent probability score ranging from 0 to 1.
L001023	An analogously mass-decorrelated \Xtoqq \ParticleNet tagger identifies hadronic vector boson decays.
L001024	To estimate the \PH mass, the ``soft-drop'' (SD) grooming algorithm~\cite{Larkoski:2014wba,Dasgupta:2013ihk,Butterworth:2008iy} with $\beta = 0$ and $z = 0.1$ is applied to AK8 jets, yielding the SD mass, \MSD.
L001025	The AK8 mass resolution is further improved using the \ParticleNet-regressed mass, \MPN.
L001026	For boosted \PH and vector boson reconstruction, AK8 jets with $\abs{\eta}<2.5$, ungroomed mass $> 50\GeV$, and $\MSD > 40\GeV$ are considered.
L001027	After selecting AK8 jets, nonoverlapping AK4 jets are used for \PQb and VBS jet identification.
L001028	The AK4 jets originating from \PQb quarks within $\abs{\eta}<2.5$ are identified using the \DeepJet~\cite{CMS:2017wtu,Bols:2020bkb,CMS-DP-2018-058} algorithm.
L001029	Jets are classified as \PQb jets based on the \DeepJet output, with loose, medium, and tight working points corresponding to light-flavor quark or gluon jet misidentification rates of 10, 1, and 0.1\%,
L001030	and efficiencies of about 92, 75, and 58\%, respectively.
L001031	The AK4 jets with $\pt > 30$\GeV and $\abs{\eta} < 4.7$ are considered as VBS jet candidates.
L001032	
L001033	We model VBS \VVH events and calculate the corresponding production cross sections at LO using the \MGvATNLO~v2.6.5~\cite{Alwall:2014hca} Monte Carlo event generator.
L001034	The generator is interfaced with \PYTHIA~v8.240~\cite{Sjostrand:2014zea} for the simulation of boson decays, using the dipole recoil scheme to correctly emulate the color structure~\cite{Cabouat:2017rzi}.
L001035	To simulate events with different \kVV values, the event reweighting feature of \MGvATNLO~\cite{mattelaer16_261} is employed.
L001036	
L001037	Background simulation is used in the optimization of the analysis, while data-driven methods are used for the purpose of modelling the data in all channels.
L001038	Simulated events are also used in the SS dilepton channel to predict the SM background, in conjunction with control samples in data.
L001039	The largest source of background in the all-hadronic channel is the production of multijet events through quantum chromodynamics (QCD) processes.
L001040	The QCD background is simulated at LO with \MGvATNLO. Other major background processes are \ttbar and \Wjets production.
L001041	The $\ttbar$ background~\cite{Frixione:2007nw} is simulated at next-to-LO (NLO) precision in perturbative QCD with the \POWHEG~v2.0 program~\cite{Nason:2004rx,Frixione:2007vw,Alioli:2010xd}.
L001042	The \Wjets background is simulated with \MGvATNLO at LO, with up to four additional partons, merged at the parton shower level via the MLM matching scheme~\cite{Alwall:2007fs}.
L001043	Other sources of background include diboson production, $s$- and $t$-channel single top quark production, electroweak production of a \PW boson, quark-initiated \WH, \ttbar~$+$~vector bosons, and \Zjets production.
L001044	
L001045	The generated matrix-element-level events are interfaced with \PYTHIA~v8.240 to model parton showering and hadronization, as well as the underlying event.
L001046	The generation of all samples is based on the CP5 tune~\cite{tuneCP5Paper} for \PYTHIA and the NNPDF~v3.1 next-to-NLO parton distribution functions (PDFs)~\cite{Ball:2014uwa,Ball:2017nwa}.
L001047	Simulated pileup interactions are added to the hard-scattering process, with a multiplicity matched to the pileup profiles of the data. The detector response is modeled with \GEANTfour~\cite{GEANT4:2002zbu}.
L001048	
L001049	The search is performed across six channels, defined according to the lepton and jet multiplicities in the event.
L001050	Events in the all-hadronic channel are required to contain exactly zero loose leptons, while in the single-lepton (dilepton) channel they must contain one (two) tight leptons.
L001051	The all-hadronic channel is further split into two categories, semi-boosted and fully boosted, which require exactly two and more than two AK8 jets, respectively.
L001052	The dilepton events are split into the OS and SS channels. Events in the OS dilepton channels are then categorized according to the dilepton mass ($m_{\Pell\Pell}$), requiring that it is either consistent with that of a \PZ boson (OS \PZ), or not (OS $\PW\PW$).
L001053	All channels are orthogonal by construction except for the SS dilepton channel. For this channel the orthogonality is explicitly verified after the event selections are applied, because of the different lepton identification criteria employed.
L001054	
L001055	In the all-hadronic channels, events of interest are collected using triggers based on the scalar \pt sum of all jets ($H_\mathrm{T}$), with thresholds of 800 or 900\GeV for 2016, and 1050\GeV for 2017 and 2018.
L001056	Events are split into the fully boosted and semi-boosted channels based on the number of AK8 jets with $\pt > 300\GeV$.
L001057	In the fully boosted channel, the \Htobb and both the vector boson candidates are reconstructed as AK8 jets.
L001058	In the semi-boosted channel, one vector boson candidate is reconstructed as a pair of AK4 jets.
L001059	In both channels, a pair of AK4 VBS jet candidates is also required: in the case of multiple pairs, the one with the largest absolute pseudorapidity difference (\absdetajj) is selected.
L001060	
L001061	In the single-lepton channel, single-lepton triggers require isolated leptons with $\pt > 24$~(27)\GeV for muons in 2016 and 2018 (2017), and $\pt > 27$~(32)\GeV for electrons in 2016 (2017--2018).
L001062	Events must contain exactly one tight lepton with $\pt > 40\GeV$ and no additional loose leptons.
L001063	Two AK8 jets with $\pt > 250\GeV$ are required and identified as the \Htobb and the hadronically decaying vector boson candidates.
L001064	The VBS jets are chosen as the highest-momentum AK4 jets in opposite $\eta$ regions.
L001065	If this criterion fails, the two highest-momentum jets are selected.
L001066	
L001067	In the dilepton channels, single-lepton triggers are used for OS $\PW\PW$ events. These are used in combination with dilepton triggers for OS \PZ events, with \pt thresholds of 17 and 8\GeV for muons, and 23 and 12\GeV for electrons.
L001068	The SS dilepton channel also uses mixed muon-electron triggers, requiring a muon (electron) with $\pt > 23$~(12)\GeV and an electron (muon) with $\pt > 23$~(8)\GeV.
L001069	Exactly two tight leptons are required, with no additional loose leptons.
L001070	In the OS \PZ channel, the two leptons must have an invariant mass $m_{\Pell\Pell}$ consistent with that of a \PZ boson, requiring $86 < m_{\Pell\Pell} < 96\GeV$, while the same window is vetoed in the OS $\PW\PW$ channel.
L001071	The \Htobb candidate is reconstructed as an AK8 jet in all channels.
L001072	In the OS \PZ channel, one vector boson decays hadronically and is also reconstructed as an AK8 jet.
L001073	The VBS jet pair is chosen as the pair of AK4 jets with the largest $\absdetajj$ in both the OS channels, while the same selection as in the single-lepton channel is used in the SS channel.
L001074	
L001075	An AK4 \PQb jet veto is applied in the single-lepton and dilepton channels, to reduce the \ttbar background contribution.
L001076	Additional selection criteria on event properties are also applied, as summarized in Tables~\ref{tab:fhfb_presel}--\ref{tab:ss_presel}\EndMatter.
L001077	
L001078	A multivariate analysis is subsequently performed in all channels.
L001079	Backgrounds are estimated from control samples in data using a matrix (``ABCD'') method~\cite{CDF:1990kbl}, except for the SS dilepton channel.
L001080	The background in the signal region (A) is predicted from data in control regions B, C, and D, defined by inverting the criteria used to select the VBS jets (B), the \VVH system (C), or both (D).
L001081	This choice of the ABCD axes is motivated by the negligible correlation expected in background events between the properties of the \VVH system and the VBS jets.
L001082	
L001083	The all-hadronic and single-lepton channels use the method suggested in Ref.~\cite{Kasieczka:2020pil} to train a deep neural network (DNN) orthogonal to the discriminating features used to select the VBS jet pair.
L001084	The output of the DNN and the VBS selection features are then used as the two ABCD axes.
L001085	Different DNNs are trained in each channel, using simulated events to separate signal in the $\kVV=2$ benchmark scenario from all background sources.
L001086	This benchmark is used as it is expected to be close to the final exclusion limit.
L001087	In the all-hadronic fully boosted topology, the DNN uses as input the \pt, $\eta$, $\phi$, and \MPN of the three selected AK8 jets, along with the invariant mass of the VBS jet pair, \Mjj.
L001088	A decorrelation term in the loss function ensures that the DNN output is independent of $\detajj$, and these same quantities are used to define the ABCD axes.
L001089	After training, the minimum thresholds used to select on the \Xtobb and \Xtoqq \ParticleNet tagger scores are tightened for both the \PH and the vector boson candidates.
L001090	The signal region A is defined by $\absdetajj > 5$ and a DNN output $>0.97$.
L001091	In the semi-boosted topology, the DNN is trained using the \pt, $\eta$, $\phi$, and \MSD of the two selected AK8 jets, the four-vectors and the invariant mass \Mjj of the AK4 jet pair forming the second vector boson candidate, and the \pt sum of all objects forming the \VVH system, \ST.
L001092	The DNN output is designed to be independent of $\detajj$.
L001093	The signal region A is defined by requiring $\absdetajj > 6$, $\Mjj > 1300\GeV$, and a DNN output $> 0.8$.
L001094	In the single-lepton channel, the DNN inputs are the \pt and \MPN of the \PH and vector boson AK8 jets, the lepton \pt, the $m_{\Pell\PQb}$ variable, defined as the minimum invariant mass of the lepton and any \PQb jet passing the loose \DeepJet tagger working point, and \ptmiss.
L001095	Its output is constructed to be orthogonal to that of a boosted decision tree (BDT) used to select the VBS jet pair, trained on the \pt and $\eta$ of the VBS jet candidates, \detajj and \Mjj.
L001096	The signal region A is defined by requiring the DNN and BDT outputs to be $>0.9$ and 0.82, respectively, with the \Xtobb (\Xtoqq) tagger score $> 0.5$ (0.7).
L001097	
L001098	In each dilepton channel, a BDT is trained to separate signal (in the $\kVV=2$ benchmark scenario) from simulated background.
L001099	The BDTs are trained using features of the \VVH system. The absence of correlation with the properties of the VBS jets is verified after the training.
L001100	In the OS $\PW\PW$ channel, inputs include the \PH candidate \pt, $\eta$, \MSD; the \pt, $\eta$, flavor of each lepton; the $\Delta\eta$, $\Delta R$, invariant mass of the two leptons; the event \ST and \ptmiss.
L001101	The signal region A requires $\Mjj > 760\GeV$, $\absdetajj > 4.9$, a BDT score $>0.85$, and an \Xtobb tagger score $>0.7$.
L001102	In the OS \PZ channel, the BDT inputs are the \pt and $\eta$ of the \PH and \PZ candidates, and of each lepton; the lepton impact parameter significances and isolation sums; and $m_{\Pell\Pell}$, the invariant mass of the \VVH system, and \ST.
L001103	The signal region A is defined by $\Mjj > 750\GeV$, $\absdetajj > 4$, and a BDT score $>0.85$.
L001104	
L001105	The selections used in each channel to define the signal region, or region A, are summarized in Table~\ref{tab:SRs}\EndMatter, where the cuts inverted to define the B, C, D regions are also specified.
L001106	
L001107	Systematic uncertainties in the background predictions are derived from closure tests in data.
L001108	An analogous ABCD method is applied in data sidebands, and the prediction is compared to the observed data in the corresponding region A of the sideband.
L001109	The uncertainty is estimated conservatively, by taking the relative difference in the worst closure sideband.
L001110	Table~\ref{tab:yields}\EndMatter shows the data yields in the regions A, B, C and D, in each channel, alongside the predicted background with its uncertainties, and the benchmark signal expected in region A, for both the $\kVV=2$ and SM scenarios.
L001111	
L001112	In the SS dilepton channel, a BDT is also trained to discriminate signal from background.
L001113	The BDT inputs include the \pt and $\eta$ of the \Htobb candidate; the \pt of the leptons; the momentum of the VBS jets and the \pt of the VBS dijet system; and $m_{\Pell\Pell}$, \ptmiss, \ST, and the scalar sum of the \pt of the leptons and \ptmiss.
L001114	The signal region is then divided into three bins according to the BDT output score ($\left[0, 0.1\right]$, $\left[0.1, 0.25\right]$, $\left[0.25, 1\right]$).
L001115	
L001116	Results are obtained via binned maximum likelihood fits of signal and background predictions to data~\cite{ParticleDataGroup:2024cfk}, using the CMS \textsc{Combine} tool~\cite{CMS:2024onh}, based on the \textsc{RooFit}~\cite{Verkerke:2003ir} and \textsc{RooStats}~\cite{Moneta:2010pm} frameworks.
L001117	In each channel, all the regions are fitted simultaneously, extracting the signal region predictions concurrently.
L001118	Systematic uncertainties are included as nuisance parameters of the likelihood function and affect the signal and background normalization, except for the SS dilepton channel where shape variations are used.
L001119	Experimental uncertainties in the signal acceptance are considered in the lepton trigger and identification efficiencies, jet energy scale and resolution~\cite{CMS:2016lmd}, \DeepJet \Pb tagging efficiency, \ParticleNet tagger efficiency, and integrated luminosity~\cite{CMS-LUM-17-003,CMS-PAS-LUM-17-004,CMS-PAS-LUM-18-002}.
L001120	Theory uncertainties in the signal cross section include renormalization and factorization scales (\muR and \muF, respectively), PDFs, and initial- and final-state radiation modeling. The overall dominant systematic uncertainty, of approximately 25\%, arises from \muF, followed by the uncertainty arising from PDFs, of approximately 10\%.
L001121	For the background prediction the statistical and closure systematic uncertainties are considered.
L001122	The sensitivity is limited mostly by the statistical uncertainty, given the low data yields in the signal regions.
L001123	
L001124	All channels are combined to obtain upper limits at 95\% confidence level (\CL) on the VBS \VVH production cross section as a function of the \kVV coupling modifier.
L001125	Assuming all other \PH couplings are equal to their values in the SM, the VBS \VVH production is excluded at 95\%~\CL for \kVV outside the observed (expected) range of $0.40 < \kVV < 1.60$ ($0.34 < \kVV < 1.66$). The results are shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:1dscan}.
L001126	
L001127	\begin{figure}[htb]
L001128	\centering
L001129	\includegraphics[width=\cmsFigWidth]{Figure_002.pdf}
L001130	\caption{
